{"slug":"top-10-biker-cafes-uk","category":"infographics","title":"10 Best Motorcycle Cafes in the UK","metaDescription":"Illustrated map of the UK's 10 best biker cafes. From the legendary Ace Cafe London to Applecross Inn in Scotland. Free to download.","heroImage":"public/images/infographics/top-10-biker-cafes-uk.png","author":"VisorUp Team","publishDate":"2026-06-09","readTime":"1 min read","tags":["infographic","shareable","cafes","destinations","UK riding"],"relatedSlugs":[],"affiliateLinks":[],"content":"<h2>10 Best Motorcycle Cafes in the UK</h2><p>Every great ride deserves a great stop. These 10 cafes are pilgrimage sites for British bikers.</p><ol><li><strong>Ace Cafe London</strong> — Stonebridge. The original since 1938.</li><li><strong>Squires Cafe</strong> — Sherburn-in-Elmet, Yorkshire. Weekend mecca.</li><li><strong>Box Hill Cafe</strong> — Surrey Hills. Sunday morning ritual.</li><li><strong>Ponderosa Cafe</strong> — Horseshoe Pass, North Wales. Mountain views.</li><li><strong>Hartside Cafe</strong> — Pennines, Cumbria. England's highest cafe.</li><li><strong>Loomies Cafe</strong> — West Meon, Hampshire. Southern favourite.</li><li><strong>Devil's Bridge Cafe</strong> — Kirkby Lonsdale, Cumbria. Dales classic.</li><li><strong>Ryka's Cafe</strong> — Box Hill, Surrey. Ducati heaven.</li><li><strong>Cafe 1000</strong> — Peak District. Cat and Fiddle essential stop.</li><li><strong>Applecross Inn</strong> — Scotland. Reward after the Bealach na Ba.</li></ol>"}
,
{"slug":"uk-mountain-passes-ranked","category":"infographics","title":"UK Top 10 Mountain Passes for Motorcyclists","metaDescription":"The 10 best UK mountain passes ranked by elevation, gradient, and difficulty for motorcycle riders. Elevation profiles and stats included.","heroImage":"public/images/infographics/uk-mountain-passes-ranked.png","author":"VisorUp Team","publishDate":"2026-06-09","readTime":"1 min read","tags":["infographic","shareable","mountain passes","UK roads","difficulty rating"],"relatedSlugs":[],"affiliateLinks":[],"content":"<h2>UK Top 10 Mountain Passes for Motorcyclists</h2><p>From England's brutal Hardknott Pass to Scotland's towering Bealach na Ba, these are the mountain roads that test riders and reward them with unforgettable views.</p><ol><li><strong>Hardknott Pass</strong> — Lake District, 393m, 33% gradient. 5/5 difficulty.</li><li><strong>Bealach na Ba</strong> — Applecross, 626m, 20% gradient. 5/5.</li><li><strong>Wrynose Pass</strong> — Lake District, 393m, 25%. 4/5.</li><li><strong>Kirkstone Pass</strong> — Lake District, 454m, 20%. 3/5.</li><li><strong>Honister Pass</strong> — Lake District, 356m, 25%. 4/5.</li><li><strong>Black Mountain Pass</strong> — Brecon Beacons, 493m, 20%. 3/5.</li><li><strong>Horseshoe Pass</strong> — North Wales, 417m, 15%. 3/5.</li><li><strong>A939 Lecht Road</strong> — Cairngorms, 635m, 15%. 3/5.</li><li><strong>A686 Hartside</strong> — Pennines, 580m, 12%. 2/5.</li><li><strong>Buttertubs Pass</strong> — Yorkshire, 526m, 15%. 2/5.</li></ol>"}
