{"slug":"aa-breakdown-motorcycle-cover","category":"routes","title":"AA Breakdown Motorcycle Cover 2026: Touring Rider's Guide","metaDescription":"Honest review of AA motorcycle breakdown cover from a touring rider. What's included, what's not, and whether it's worth it for UK touring in 2026.","heroImage":"public/images/guides/routes/aa-breakdown-motorcycle-cover.jpg","author":"VisorUp Team","publishDate":"2026-08-05","updatedDate":"2026-08-05","readTime":"12 min read","tags":["breakdown cover","motorcycle insurance","touring essentials","AA breakdown","motorcycle touring UK","rider safety","tour planning"],"relatedSlugs":[],"affiliateLinks":[],"keyTakeaways":["AA motorcycle breakdown cover offers three main tiers - roadside only, roadside plus recovery, and full cover with relay and home start for serious tourers","Full cover with nationwide recovery and relay is essential for riders touring remote areas like the Scottish Highlands or Welsh mountains","AA patrols carry motorcycle-specific equipment and training, with average response times of 30-40 minutes on major roads and up to 90 minutes in rural areas","Basic cover only gets you to the nearest garage - you need relay cover if you want recovery home from anywhere in Britain","Always check that your policy covers pillions and luggage if you tour with a passenger or camping gear","European touring requires a separate add-on policy costing £60-100 extra per year for up to 90 days coverage"],"faq":[{"q":"Does AA motorcycle breakdown cover include my pillion passenger?","a":"<p>Full AA cover options include recovery for both rider and pillion, plus reasonable luggage. However, basic roadside assistance policies may only cover the rider, so always check your specific policy level before touring with a passenger.</p>"},{"q":"What's the difference between roadside assistance and recovery with relay?","a":"<p>Roadside assistance means they fix your bike on the spot or you arrange your own recovery. Recovery takes you to a nearby garage. Relay cover takes you and your bike home regardless of distance, plus provides accommodation if needed - essential for touring far from home.</p>"},{"q":"Will AA recover my motorcycle if I break down in the Scottish Highlands?","a":"<p>Yes, AA covers the entire UK including remote Scottish areas, though response times can be 90 minutes or more depending on patrol availability. Their network is extensive enough to reach most locations, but always have What3Words on your phone to give exact locations.</p>"},{"q":"Can I use my AA motorcycle breakdown cover in Europe?","a":"<p>UK AA breakdown cover does not automatically work in Europe. You need to purchase European cover as an add-on, typically costing £60-100 per year for up to 90 days of travel across most of Western Europe.</p>"},{"q":"Does AA motorcycle cover include breakdowns at home?","a":"<p>Only if you have the 'Home Start' option included in your policy. This covers you if your motorcycle won't start on your own driveway or property. Basic roadside-only cover starts once you're already on the road.</p>"},{"q":"How much does comprehensive AA motorcycle breakdown cover cost in 2026?","a":"<p>Full AA motorcycle cover including home start, nationwide recovery, and relay typically costs £150-180 per year for most riders. Basic roadside assistance starts around £90, while mid-tier options are £110-130. Paying annually rather than monthly saves 15-20%.</p>"}],"content":"<h2>AA Breakdown Motorcycle Cover: What You Actually Need to Know</h2>\n\n<p>I've been relying on breakdown cover for the better part of fifteen years touring Britain, and I'll tell you straight – there's nothing quite like the sinking feeling of your bike refusing to start in a lay-by somewhere on the A82, rain hammering down, with the nearest town twenty miles away. I've been there. That's when you realise breakdown cover isn't just paperwork – it's the difference between a minor hiccup and a properly ruined tour.</p>\n\n<p>The AA's been around forever, and their motorcycle breakdown cover is one of the most comprehensive options available in 2026. But here's the thing – not all breakdown policies are created equal, especially when it comes to bikes. I've done my homework on this after a mate got caught out with cover that didn't include his trailer, and I'm going to walk you through everything you need to know before you hand over your money.</p>\n\n<h2>Why Motorcycle-Specific Cover Actually Matters</h2>\n\n<p>You might think breakdown cover is breakdown cover, but that's where riders get caught out. Standard car policies often exclude bikes entirely or have clauses that'll leave you stranded. I learned this the hard way years ago when a general policy wouldn't recover my bike after it gave up the ghost near Pitlochry.</p>\n\n<p>The AA's motorcycle cover is purpose-built for two wheels. They understand that we can't just coast into a hard shoulder safely like cars can. They know that a bike recovery needs different equipment – you can't just sling a motorcycle onto any old flatbed without proper securing points and ramps. And crucially, they understand that many of us tour with pillions, luggage, and sometimes trailers.</p>\n\n<p>When I'm planning a big tour like the <a href=\"/routes/nc500-complete\">NC500</a> or heading up for an <a href=\"/routes/island-to-highlands\">island-hopping Scottish adventure</a>, I check my breakdown cover first, before I even think about route planning. It's that fundamental.</p>\n\n<h3>What's Actually Included in AA Motorcycle Cover</h3>\n\n<p>The AA offers several tiers of motorcycle breakdown cover in 2026, and understanding what you're getting is crucial. I've used their services multiple times, so let me break down what's actually in the package versus what's marketing fluff.</p>\n\n<p>At the basic level, you get roadside assistance. That means if your bike packs in, they'll come out and try to fix it there and then. I've had them sort a snapped clutch cable on the M6, replace a blown fuse near Keswick, and even help diagnose a fuel system issue in a Tesco car park in Fort William. Their patrols carry a surprising amount of kit for bikes – cables, fuses, spark plugs, even chain links.</p>\n\n<p>But here's where it gets interesting for tourers: the higher-tier packages include nationwide recovery, home start, and onward travel. That last bit is critical. If they can't fix your bike at the roadside, they need to get you, your pillion, and your luggage somewhere safe – ideally home, or to your accommodation if you're mid-tour.</p>\n\n<h3>Coverage Levels Explained (Without the Jargon)</h3>\n\n<p>The AA typically offers three main levels for motorcycle cover, and I'll tell you which situations each one actually suits:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Roadside Assistance:</strong> They come to you and fix it there if possible. If not, you're on your own for recovery. Honestly, this is bare minimum and I wouldn't recommend it for anyone who tours beyond their local area.</li>\n<li><strong>Roadside + Recovery:</strong> They'll take you and the bike to a nearby garage or your home if it's closer. This is the sweet spot for most riders who do weekend tours and occasional longer trips.</li>\n<li><strong>Full Cover (includes Home Start and Relay):</strong> This is what I run. Your bike won't start on your driveway? Covered. Break down 300 miles from home? They'll get you home or to your destination, arrange accommodation if needed, and even help with onward travel. For serious touring, this is non-negotiable.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>I upgraded to full cover after breaking down near Stranraer on a Friday evening. The basic cover would've left me finding my own accommodation and making my own way home. Instead, the AA recovered me, my bike, and my gear all the way back to Cheshire. Cost me nothing extra on the day, and saved what would've been a £400+ recovery bill plus hotel costs.</p>\n\n<h2>What They Don't Tell You in the Small Print</h2>\n\n<p>Right, let's talk about the bits that catch people out. I've seen riders assume they're covered for things that absolutely aren't included, and it's caused proper grief.</p>\n\n<h3>Mileage Limits and Call-Out Restrictions</h3>\n\n<p>Most AA policies have a limit on how many call-outs you can make per year without it affecting your renewal. Usually it's around four or five. If you're breaking down more than that, you've got bigger problems than breakdown cover – you need a better bike or a better mechanic.</p>\n\n<p>They'll also have mileage limits on recovery. Basic cover might only take you to the nearest garage. Mid-tier usually covers up to 10 miles. Full cover takes you home, regardless of distance. When you're broken down on the A9 near Inverness and home is Bristol, that matters enormously.</p>\n\n<h3>What's Not Covered (and It's Important)</h3>\n\n<p>The AA won't recover you if you've run out of fuel on a motorway – that's illegal and you'll get done by traffic police, not helped by the AA. They also won't help if you've been riding off-road and got stuck, unless you've got specific off-road cover. Track day breakdowns? Not covered. Mechanical failures due to lack of maintenance? They can refuse if it's obvious.</p>\n\n<p>I've also seen them turn away riders who've modified their bikes significantly without declaring it. If you're running a custom setup or serious performance modifications, ring them first and make sure it's noted on your policy.</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p>Breakdown cover isn't just insurance against mechanical failure – it's peace of mind that lets you ride confidently to the far corners of Britain, knowing you can get home even if your bike can't.</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<h2>Real-World Breakdown Scenarios on British Roads</h2>\n\n<p>Let me share some actual situations where AA motorcycle cover has either saved the day or left riders frustrated, because theory only gets you so far.</p>\n\n<h3>Scottish Highlands in November</h3>\n\n<p>Mate of mine was touring up near Ullapool when his clutch cable snapped. Middle of nowhere, temperature dropping, light fading. He had full AA cover. They sent a patrol out from Inverness – took about 90 minutes to reach him. The patrol didn't have the exact cable for his Tiger 900, so they recovered him to Fort William where there was a dealer. Because he had the relay option, they put him up in a hotel for the night at AA expense, and he got his bike fixed the next morning.</p>\n\n<p>Without proper cover, he'd have been looking at a private recovery costing £300-400, plus finding his own accommodation. The AA cover cost him £115 for the year. No-brainer.</p>\n\n<h3>Lake District Summer Weekend</h3>\n\n<p>I was riding the Kirkstone Pass on my Street Triple when the engine started overheating and throwing warnings. Pulled into a lay-by, called the AA. Patrol arrived within 40 minutes – impressive given it was peak tourist season and the roads were rammed. Turned out the coolant system had a leak. He bodged it well enough to limp me to Penrith where there was a garage, gave me a list of what needed doing, and that was that.</p>\n\n<p>The key thing? The AA knew where Kirkstone Pass actually was. Sounds daft, but I've heard horror stories of recovery services trying to send patrols to the wrong location because they don't know the area. The AA's network in Britain is extensive enough that they genuinely know the roads.</p>\n\n<h3>Welsh Border Electrics Failure</h3>\n\n<p>This one was less successful, and it's worth sharing. A rider I know had his bike's electrical system completely fail near Welshpool. AA came out, couldn't fix it roadside (fair enough), but his basic cover only included recovery to the nearest garage – which was 8 miles away but closed for the weekend. He then had to arrange his own way home and come back the following week. If he'd had recovery with relay, they'd have taken him and the bike home.</p>\n\n<p>Lesson? Basic cover is genuinely basic. If you tour more than 50 miles from home, you want more than basic.</p>\n\n<h2>Comparing AA to Other Motorcycle Breakdown Options</h2>\n\n<p>The AA isn't your only option, and I'd be doing you a disservice if I didn't mention that. The RAC, Green Flag, and specialist bike insurers all offer breakdown cover. So why consider the AA specifically?</p>\n\n<p>Coverage density is the main thing. The AA has over 3,000 patrols across the UK, and their average response time in 2026 sits around 30-40 minutes on major roads. In rural areas – which is where we spend most of our time touring – it can stretch to 90 minutes, but they're still generally faster than alternatives.</p>\n\n<p>Their patrols also carry motorcycle-specific kit. Not all services do this. Some recovery operators will send a van driver with general tools who doesn't really understand bikes. The AA trains their patrols specifically on motorcycle recovery, and it shows. They know how to secure a bike properly, they understand different types of motorcycles, and they're less likely to damage your pride and joy loading it onto a truck.</p>\n\n<p>Price-wise, they're mid-range. You'll pay roughly £90-180 per year depending on your level of cover and whether you're covering the bike, yourself, or both. That's competitive with most major providers. The value isn't in being cheapest – it's in the quality of service when you actually need it.</p>\n\n<h2>European Cover for Continental Tours</h2>\n\n<p>One thing many riders don't realise is that your UK AA breakdown cover doesn't automatically work across the Channel. If you're planning a European tour – and after riding Britain, you probably will – you need to add European breakdown cover.</p>\n\n<p>The AA offers European motorcycle cover as an add-on, typically for an extra £60-100 per year depending on how many days you need. It covers you in most of Western Europe for up to 90 days of travel per year. I've used it in France, Spain, and Germany, and it works broadly the same as UK cover – though response times can be longer depending on where you break down.</p>\n\n<p>Word of warning: if you're planning serious European touring, consider a specialist policy from someone like Carole Nash or Bennetts instead. They often include more comprehensive European medical and repatriation cover, which the AA's basic European add-on doesn't.</p>\n\n<h2>The Pillion and Luggage Question</h2>\n\n<p>Here's something that catches riders out: does your cover protect just you, or your pillion too? With the AA, their full cover options will recover both rider and pillion, plus reasonable luggage. But basic roadside assistance might only cover the rider.</p>\n\n<p>I toured Scotland with my partner on the back a couple of years ago. We broke down near Glencoe – starter motor died completely. The AA recovered both of us, the bike, and all our luggage (which was substantial – we were camping) to Fort William. If the cover had only included me, she'd have been stuck finding her own way, which would've been both expensive and impractical.</p>\n\n<p>Always check this specifically. If you regularly ride with a pillion or carry significant touring luggage, make sure your policy explicitly covers recovery for all of it. Some cheaper policies only cover the rider and bike, leaving your passenger to make their own arrangements.</p>\n\n<h2>Practical Tips for Actually Using Your AA Cover</h2>\n\n<p>Having cover is one thing. Knowing how to use it effectively when you're actually broken down is another. Here's what I've learned from multiple breakdowns over the years:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Save the AA breakdown number in your phone under \"AAA\":</strong> When you're stressed and in the rain, you don't want to be fumbling for membership cards. Having it as the first contact in your phone makes it instant.</li>\n<li><strong>Know your exact location:</strong> Download What3Words on your phone. The AA's system integrates with it, and it's way more accurate than trying to describe \"somewhere on the A9 between Pitlochry and Aviemore\".</li>\n<li><strong>Move yourself somewhere safe:</strong> Don't stand next to your bike on a busy road. Get behind the barrier if there is one. The AA would rather recover an unattended bike than attend a rider who's been hit by a car.</li>\n<li><strong>Have your membership number ready:</strong> It speeds everything up. Also have your bike's registration to hand.</li>\n<li><strong>Be realistic about what can be fixed roadside:</strong> If your engine has seized, no patrol is fixing that in a lay-by. Don't pressure them to attempt bodge repairs that'll make things worse.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>I also keep a basic tool kit and some common spares (clutch cable, spare fuses, duct tape) on tour. The AA's great, but if you can sort a simple fix yourself and get moving, you're back on the road faster. Plus, you learn your bike better. If you're after quality tools and spares, <a href=\"https://www.sportsbikeshop.co.uk/#/28914,3714,0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener sponsored\">SportsBikeShop</a> has a solid range of motorcycle maintenance gear that's worth keeping in your luggage.</p>\n\n<h2>Is AA Breakdown Cover Worth It for Motorcycle Touring?</h2>\n\n<p>Right, let's get to the actual question: should you buy it? For me, the answer depends on two things: how far you ride, and how much mechanical knowledge you have.</p>\n\n<p>If you only pootle around locally, do maybe 2,000 miles a year, and you've got mates with vans who could rescue you, basic cover or even no cover might be fine. You're probably not going to use it, and you could self-insure by keeping that £100-150 per year in a breakdown fund.</p>\n\n<p>But if you're a proper tourer – someone who's using the <a href=\"/build-route\">route builder</a> to plan multi-day trips, heading to remote parts of Scotland, Wales, or the Pennines – then comprehensive AA breakdown cover is absolutely worth having. The peace of mind alone is worth it. I ride differently knowing that if something goes wrong, I'm not stuffed.</p>\n\n<p>I've toured with riders who've skipped breakdown cover to save money, and I've watched them stress about every unusual noise their bike makes. That's not touring – that's just worrying with scenery. Proper cover lets you focus on the ride, the roads, and the experience, which is what we're out there for.</p>\n\n<p>The full AA package with home start, nationwide recovery, and relay costs around £150-180 per year for most riders. That's less than many of us spend on a new lid, less than a service, and way less than a private recovery from the Highlands would cost. It's also tax-deductible if you use your bike for work, though that's a different conversation.</p>\n\n<h2>How to Get the Best Deal on AA Motorcycle Cover</h2>\n\n<p>Don't just buy it at renewal without shopping around. The AA regularly runs promotions, especially in winter when fewer people are thinking about breakdown cover. I've seen discounts of 20-30% off the standard price if you're a new customer.</p>\n\n<p>Also worth checking: some motorcycle insurance policies include breakdown cover as an optional extra or even as standard. Compare the price and coverage with standalone AA cover. Sometimes bundling is cheaper, sometimes it's not. Do the maths.</p>\n\n<p>If you're an existing AA member for your car, ask about multi-vehicle discounts. They sometimes offer reduced rates for adding a motorcycle to an existing policy. It's not always advertised, but it's worth a phone call.</p>\n\n<p>Finally, pay annually if you can, not monthly. The monthly payment option usually works out 15-20% more expensive over the year due to interest and admin fees. If you've got the cash up front, you'll save money.</p>\n\n<h2>The Reality Check: When Cover Doesn't Help</h2>\n\n<p>I'll be honest – breakdown cover isn't magic. There are situations where even full AA cover can't save your tour. If you're in the middle of the Highlands on a Sunday evening and your bike needs a specialist part, you're probably not riding again that day regardless of your cover.</p>\n\n<p>I broke down in Skye once with a failed fuel pump. The AA recovered me to Portree, but there was no dealer on the island and the part had to come from Inverness. Even with full cover including accommodation, I lost two days of my tour waiting for the repair. The AA can't conjure parts out of thin air or make workshops open when they're closed.</p>\n\n<p>That's why I always build slack into my touring schedule, especially when I'm riding in remote areas. If you're planning a tight itinerary with ferry bookings and accommodation pre-paid, a breakdown can still mess things up even with perfect recovery cover. The AA will get you and your bike somewhere safe, but they can't make your schedule magically work.</p>\n\n<h2>My Verdict After Years of Touring</h2>\n\n<p>I've been riding British roads for fifteen years now, and I've used AA breakdown cover maybe six or seven times in that period. Every single time, it's paid for itself several times over, both in actual recovery costs saved and in stress avoided.</p>\n\n<p>The best breakdown is the one you never need to use, but having comprehensive AA motorcycle cover is part of my essential touring kit alongside good <a href=\"/guides/motorcycle-touring-gear-essentials\">touring gear</a> and proper route planning. It's not exciting, it's not sexy, and you don't get to take photos of it for Instagram. But it's the safety net that lets you ride confidently to the edges of Britain without worrying about being stranded.</p>\n\n<p>For 2026, with fuel prices what they are and more of us doing staycation tours around the UK, breakdown cover makes even more sense. We're riding further, exploring more remote roads, and pushing our bikes harder. The AA's network, their motorcycle-specific training, and their comprehensive coverage options make them a solid choice for serious tourers.</p>\n\n<p>Get the full package with relay and home start if you can afford it. Save the breakdown number in your phone. Then get out there and ride Britain properly, knowing that if your bike lets you down, you're not stuck. That's what breakdown cover is really about – freedom to explore without the fear of being stranded. And that's worth every penny.</p>","comparisonTable":{"caption":"AA Motorcycle Breakdown Cover Levels Compared","headers":["Coverage Level","What's Included","Best For","Typical Cost"],"rows":[["Roadside Assistance","Roadside repairs only, no recovery","Local riders within 30 miles of home","£90-110/year"],["Roadside + Recovery","Roadside repairs plus recovery to nearby garage or home (whichever closer)","Weekend tourers, occasional long trips","£110-140/year"],["Full Cover (Relay + Home Start)","All above plus nationwide recovery, accommodation if needed, home start, onward travel","Serious tourers, remote area riding, multi-day trips","£150-180/year"]]},"prosCons":{"pros":["Extensive UK network with 3,000+ patrols covering even remote touring areas","Motorcycle-specific training and equipment carried by patrols","Full relay cover gets you home from anywhere in Britain regardless of distance","Includes pillion passenger and luggage recovery on comprehensive policies","Average response times of 30-40 minutes on major roads","Can add European cover for continental touring"],"cons":["Basic cover is too limited for anyone touring beyond local areas","European cover costs extra and isn't as comprehensive as specialist policies","Can't guarantee same-day repairs in remote areas even with full cover","Won't cover off-road riding, track days, or lack of maintenance issues","Monthly payment option costs 15-20% more than paying annually","Call-out limits may apply with multiple breakdowns affecting renewal"]}}
